WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
The Amazon and the Sahara are both hot.

2
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.620
So why is one a rainforest, and the other a desert?

3
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:16.313
Each is a biome, a large region with similar climate and similar plants and animals.

4
00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:20.393
To guess a biome, ask two things of every month.

5
00:00:21.573 --> 00:00:24.293
How warm is it? How wet?

6
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:26.557
Start with warmth.

7
00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:31.417
Shine a torch straight down at a table, and you get a small bright spot.

8
00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:36.077
Tilt it, and the same light spreads into a larger, dimmer oval.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.500
Sunlight does the same on a round Earth.

10
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.500
Averaged over a year, it strikes the equator most directly, and the poles at a

11
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.040
slant. So the tropics get the most energy per square metre, and the poles the least.

12
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.833
But the Amazon and the Sahara get similar average warmth.

13
00:00:57.053 --> 00:01:00.313
What differs is water, and moving air decides where it falls.

14
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:03.907
Over the warm equator, moist air rises.

15
00:01:05.247 --> 00:01:07.787
As it rises, it expands and cools.

16
00:01:08.787 --> 00:01:11.887
Its water vapour condenses into cloud, and it rains.

17
00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:15.287
This is the equatorial rain belt.

18
00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:19.437
The air, now dry, flows toward the poles high up.

19
00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:23.797
Near 30° degrees north and south, it sinks back down.

20
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.310
Sinking air is squeezed, and it warms.

21
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.810
Warming takes it further from the point where its vapour condenses.

22
00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.990
So sinking air stays clear, and rain is rare.

23
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.530
Many great deserts lie beneath it, the Sahara among them.

24
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:43.703
So here is the first half of the rule.

25
00:01:45.103 --> 00:01:47.623
Rain falls where moist air rises and cools.

26
00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:50.643
It is scarce where air sinks and warms.

27
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:53.557
The rain belt does not stay put.

28
00:01:54.777 --> 00:01:56.857
Through the year, it shifts north and south.

29
00:01:58.157 --> 00:02:01.477
It roughly follows the overhead Sun, but lags behind it.

30
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.630
Picture a town some way north of the equator, near the edge of that swing.

31
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.450
Does it get rain all year, or only part of it?

32
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:23.267
Only part of it. The town is wet while the rain belt is overhead, and dry when the

33
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:27.147
belt moves away. A wet season, then a dry one.

34
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.730
Ecologists draw that on a climate diagram.

35
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.530
Months run along the bottom.

36
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.250
A red line shows temperature.

37
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:38.750
A blue line shows rain.

38
00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:44.373
The scales are linked, so 10 °C sits level with 20 mm.

39
00:02:45.713 --> 00:02:49.213
When the blue line drops below the red, the month is read as dry.

40
00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:52.503
That is the second half of the rule.

41
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:56.803
The warmer a month, the more rain it needs to count as humid.

42
00:02:57.917 --> 00:03:02.177
Read month by month, warmth and water give a first guess at the biome.

43
00:03:03.437 --> 00:03:05.037
A guess, not a final answer.
